Ava: Quick ask — I'm putting together a short customer-facing cheat-sheet for handling small onboarding surges (1–3k qps spikes) for accounts at renewal risk. Want something field-friendly: steps to reduce perceived latency and keep cost predictable. Draft below, feedback?

Liam: Love it. Keep it prescriptive and tiny — 3 steps max. customers read 3 bullets. :ok_hand:

Ava: Agreed. Draft v0: 1) Warm key prefixes (prefill top 10 user prompts) 2) Turn on lightweight batching for short requests (<= 256 tokens) 3) Fall back to low-cost quantized variant for non-critical routes.

Maya: Add a quick config snippet for the SDK to enable batching + TTL cache. Customers like copy-paste.

Ava: good call — adding snippet.

Maya: Snippet (JS):
```js
// client config: enable batching + small prefetch TTL
const client = new Redwood.Client({
  batching: { enabled: true, maxBatchSize: 8, maxLatencyMs: 20 },
  cache: { enabled: true, prefixTTLSeconds: 300 }
});
```

Riley: For SRE/ops note: recommend a warmup schedule in the first 10 minutes after go-live: 200 warm requests over 5m, staggered by 1.5s — avoids cold starts + KV cache misses. Also include a health-check curl that checks both model and cache hit rate.

Sam: Can we add a one-liner customers can paste into their prod pipeline to prefill? e.g. curl to submit prefill?

Ava: Yup — quick curl to prefill top prompts (replace tenant_id + api_key):
```bash
curl -X POST https://api.redwood.example/v1/prefill \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $API_KEY" \
  -d '{"tenant_id":"T-123","prompts":["Welcome message","Order status","FAQ 1"]}'
```

Liam: Also include a fallback checklist for when latency spikes persist: 1) reduce batch window to 10ms, 2) switch non-critical endpoints to "cheap-variant-v2" model, 3) enable streaming so UI gets tokens asap.

Ava: I'll add that as a short flowchart line.

Riley: Ops tip: expose two metrics to the customer in the playbook: model_latency_p50 and cache_hit_ratio. Show acceptable thresholds: p50 < 120ms, cache_hit_ratio > 0.55. If cache_hit_ratio < 0.4 -> investigate prefix mismatch.

Maya: For engineers: recommended quantization + batch settings for text-heavy endpoints: quantized-8 + maxBatchSize 6 for sub-256 token responses. That keeps cost down with small perf hit.

Sam: Add a short troubleshooting Q&A: e.g. "If customers see low hit rate -> check prompt canonicalization (lowercase + trim) and prefix keys; if streaming stutters -> increase chunkSize to 64."

Ava: Perfect. One more: include a short script customers can run to generate the 200 warm requests (node):
```js
// warmup.js
const prompts = ["Welcome","Help","Order status"];
async function warm() {
  for (let i=0;i<200;i++) {
    await client.generate({prompt: prompts[i%prompts.length]});
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 1500));
  }
}
warm();
```

Liam: This looks customer-ready. One ask — add a line about expectations: "Prefill + warmup usually brings latency down in 5–12 minutes; if not, open a support ticket and we'll run a traceset."

Ava: Adding. Also tagging prod-bot to create a short KB: /kb create "Surge mitigation for small onboarding bursts" and attach this snippet.

prod-bot: KB created: "Surge mitigation for onboarding bursts" — ID KB-4472. Link: https://kb.redwood.example/kb-4472

Ava: Thanks all — I'll publish the cheat-sheet and share with field ops. If anyone wants edits after it's live, ping me. :rocket:

Sam: FYI I flagged BrightWave as a candidate — going live tomorrow. I'll paste KB link in the handoff doc.

Riley: For BrightWave SRE: I'll run a quick capacity smoke test at 09:00 PT tomorrow and leave results in the handoff doc.

Ava: Awesome. Closing thread after publish.
